WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:06.529
1995's Toy Story is arguably Pixar's most
beloved film for both kids and adults, kicking

00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:09.000
off a sprawling franchise that's endured the
test of time.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.919
As much as kids might love seeing their favorite
toys have adventures, however, rewatching

00:00:12.919 --> 00:00:17.619
Toy Story as a grown-up reveals the film as
a dense, sometimes dark movie with more layers

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.820
than one could have ever imagined as a little
kid.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.080
Animators filled Toy Story with subtle gags,
Easter eggs, and cultural references that

00:00:25.089 --> 00:00:28.609
very few children would understand, but that
their parents, and even grandparents, might

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:29.680
get a kick out of.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:33.760
For example, the character of Mr. Potato Head
is voiced by the late, great insult comedian

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.221
Don Rickles, who was at the height of his
career in stand-up in the '60s and '70s.

00:00:38.221 --> 00:00:41.480
At one point, Mr. Potato Head makes a crack
at one of Andy's other toys:

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.390
"You uncultured swine!

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:45.640
What are you looking at, ya hockey puck?"

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:51.280
Referring to someone as a "hockey puck," which
definitely sounds like something you can't

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.700
say on TV, was Rickles' signature line.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.840
On the other end of the spectrum is the super
creepy carpet in Sid's house.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.400
How can a carpet be creepy?

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.820
Because it's the same pattern as the carpet
of the nightmarish Overlook Hotel in the 1980

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:08.320
horror classic The Shining, where we see Danny
playing with his toys.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.240
Come and play with Woody and Buzz… forever.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:17.220
Something very intimate is definitely going
on between Woody and Bo Peep, and while kids

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:22.150
might take their conversations at face value,
adults in the know recognize it as pretty

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:22.700
suggestive.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:27.580
"What do you say I get someone else to watch
the sheep tonight?…"

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:35.620
What exactly they're going to do is up for
debate, given traditional toy anatomy, but

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:40.160
there's no doubt about what's going on here:
Bo Peep is pre-arranging a booty call.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.979
Another risqué joke requires a bit more unpacking.

00:01:42.979 --> 00:01:47.580
One of Sid's freaky frankentoys is named Legs,
and she consists of what appears to be the

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:51.090
long, shapely legs of a Barbie doll connected
and a hook on a line.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.280
In other words, Legs is a "hooker."

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:58.860
Woody is a kind cowboy in the vein of classic
good-guy cowpokes like Roy Rogers and Gene

00:01:58.860 --> 00:01:59.360
Autry.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.400
One thing that sets him apart is that although
he's the hero of the movie, Woody makes a

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.240
lot of attempts to get rid of his new rival…
permanently.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:10.980
Woody moves to get rid of perceived interloper
Buzz Lightyear, intending to knock him behind

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:12.820
a piece of furniture so he'll get lost.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.560
He instead knocks him out of an open window,
where there is little chance he'll ever be

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:17.060
found.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:21.500
Even worse: he grabs the remote of R.C., the
remote-controlled car, and forces that toy

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:25.950
to be the one to nudge Buzz, thereby making
R.C. an unwilling accomplice.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:29.959
Usually in Disney movies, it's the bad guy
who tries to kill the good guy; in Toy Story,

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.820
the "good" guy tries to kill the other good
guy.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:38.120
Toy Story is a movie for kids, but some of
its themes are much more familiar to adults,

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:40.810
particularly those who struggle with mental
illness.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:44.999
At its core, it's a movie about anxiety, delusions,
and the disappointment of believing you're

00:02:44.999 --> 00:02:46.650
not living up to your potential.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:50.280
For the first half of the film, Buzz Lightyear
believes that he's not just a toy, but that

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.400
he really is an actual space ranger.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:53.280
"YOU!

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:53.960
ARE!

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:54.720
A!

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:55.900
TOY!"

00:02:56.180 --> 00:03:00.340
He is so firmly under that delusion that he
cannot see his reality for what it is—he

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:04.120
hangs around self-aware toys and lives in
a child's room (and even freezes when real

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:07.180
humans come around) but still does not waver
in his stance.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.379
The only thing that makes Buzz realize what
he truly is comes when he sees a TV commercial

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:15.580
for Buzz Lightyear toys, and in it sees rows
and rows of himself on a toy store shelf.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:19.040
Buzz immediately, and understandably, becomes
quite depressed.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:22.940
Also dealing with an identity crisis is Woody,
the film's other main character.

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:27.379
His main motivation for getting rid of Buzz
is to remain the dominant toy in Andy's heart

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:28.280
and toy box.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.849
An old-fashioned toy, he is extremely threatened
by the shiny, high-tech Buzz Lightyear toy

00:03:32.849 --> 00:03:35.830
swooping in and stealing his place as Andy's
favorite.

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:40.249
He's like a cowboy doll version of Willy Loman
from Death of a Salesman, desperately trying

00:03:40.249 --> 00:03:44.269
to hold on to relevance and usefulness in
a changing world that finds him increasingly

00:03:44.269 --> 00:03:44.760
obsolete.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:49.879
The toys of Toy Story are living things, of
course, because they clearly walk, talk, think,

00:03:49.879 --> 00:03:53.330
and feel, but that isn't the same as being
an actual biological organism.

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:57.170
The main difference between the toys and animals,
for example, is that they cannot die in a

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:57.860
normal fashion.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:03:59.980
They are simply played with or not played
with.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:01.660
If they are played with, great.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:06.860
If not, they sit on a shelf, or in a toy box
or some other storage option indefinitely,

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:11.150
aware of the passage of time as they wait
and wait and wait to be played with, a moment

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:12.100
which may never come.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.660
We definitely know that toys can die, though,
since Sid blows up a Combat Carl action figure

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:21.540
and Buzz nearly meets his end in a similar
way, as Sid ties a small rocket to his back.

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:26.520
Even worse, Sid's homemade chimeras are also
alive, Frankensteined together from other

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:27.520
dismembered toys.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:32.860
So toys can die, but the only means by which
to kill them is with brutal, painful torture,

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:34.520
and even that might not end their suffering.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:35.700
Now that's dark.

